{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. Raw HTML means an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes; text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "The core implementation and tests are solid, but one part of the code does not follow the Markdown rule, and no `{impl}` marker covers it.\n\n**What works**\n- `protected_ranges` in `src/lib.rs` uses pulldown-cmark offset events. It protects code blocks (fenced and indented), inline code spans, HTML blocks and comments (`Event::Html`), and inline tags including attributes (`Event::InlineHtml`).\n  - Text between inline tags stays ordinary Markdown, as the requirement says.\n- `reference_roles` skips any `{` that `is_escaped` (odd count of preceding backslashes) or `is_protected`. It applies this only when `markdown` is true.\n- `markdown_fences` in `src/fenced.rs` marks fence lines that overlap protected ranges, so `:::{cite}` examples inside code stay literal.\n- `definition_blocks` ignores `:::{req}` openers and closers inside code blocks.\n  - Legacy inline `{req}` roles never match, because `reference_roles` only recognises impl, cite, test and term.\n- `is_markdown_path` (`src/outline/profile.rs`) is case-insensitive and covers `md` and `markdown`. `collect_references_with_progress` uses it to decide whether a file is Markdown.\n- Tests:\n  - `roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal` covers an HTML block, a block comment, an inline comment, an attribute value and text between tags, plus the non-Markdown contrast.\n  - `markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal` covers an inline code span, a fenced block and an escaped role.\n  - `code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal` covers legacy `{req}` and a `{req}` directive inside a fence.\n  - `every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal` covers `.md`, `.markdown`, `.MD`, `.Markdown` and a `.txt` contrast.\n  - `references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown` and `colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal` cover requirement bodies and legacy roles.\n\n**Gap**\n- `direct_references` in `src/terms.rs` decides \"is Markdown\" with its own test, `file.ends_with(\".md\")`, and passes the result to `reference_roles`.\n  - Every other site uses `is_markdown_path`, which treats `.markdown`, `.MD` and `.Markdown` as Markdown too.\n  - For an outline input such as `notes.markdown` or `NOTES.MD`, `{term}` roles in inline code, fenced code, raw HTML or escaped form are scanned as ordinary text. They count as term references, which can add spurious dependencies and \"no matching named definition\" errors. That contradicts \"In Markdown, … MUST NOT count as references.\"\n  - `direct_references` carries only `requirements.definitions.dependencies` and `requirements.definitions.selection` markers, not `requirements.references.markdown_literals`.\n  - No test covers a non-`.md` Markdown extension for term-dependency scanning. The extension test only exercises `collect_references`.\n- This is a real inconsistency that no source comment or test acknowledges, so the result is false rather than FIXME.\n- Minor: no test covers a double backslash (`\\\\{impl}`, where the role is not escaped).\n\nFixing it is small: use `is_markdown_path` in `src/terms.rs`, mark it as an implementation of this requirement, and add a test with a `.markdown` input.",
  "references": "75fe2e9a32f2ecbcc2054d3888b3449f0d9f42a1c4101216f92460e087751e02",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.references.markdown_literals",
